  ARGUE: coherence with stated principles, precedent, tradeoff acknowledgment
  ATTACK VIA: coherence challenge, analogical breakdown, generalization failure
  PREMISES: "In certain domains such as criminal justice risk scoring and benefits adjudication, deployed AI systems contaminate the very feedback data that would be used to evaluate their accuracy, making post-deployment auditing structurally unreliable" — attackable; "Independent ground truth against which to measure AI system performance is absent or inaccessible in these domains, unlike domains with verifiable physical measurements" — attackable
  VULNERABLE: Even in feedback-contaminated domains, well-designed randomized holdout experiments, synthetic counterfactual evaluation, or structured adversarial red-teaming could restore meaningful post-deployment verification, potentially making pre-deployment certification an unnecessarily rigid and innovation-chilling gate when lighter-weight ongoing monitoring with mandatory experimental protocols could achieve equivalent safety assurance at lower cost.

